<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Çineköy inscription</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Çineköy inscription is a bilingual text in Luwian hieroglyphs and Phoenician carved on the base of a statue of the storm god found in 1997 at Çineköy, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adana">Adana</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. Dating to the late 8th century BCE, it was set up by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awariku">Warika</a> (Awariku), king of Hiyawa, and refers to his close ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>. It confirmed that Hiyawa was the Luwian name of the kingdom the Assyrians called Que.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cinekoy-inscription.jpg" alt="Çineköy inscription" width="320" height="514" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Klaus-Peter Simon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AdanaMuseumCinek%C3%B6y.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
